# Executive decision

<div class="decision">
**Recommended primary strategy:** analyse measured BMI and measured waist circumference as continuous adiposity outcomes in separate survey-weighted linear interaction models. This follows the Project 1 advice to avoid dichotomising BMI and recognises waist circumference as a complementary measure of central adiposity. BMI-defined obesity and sex-specific elevated waist circumference are retained as clinically interpretable sensitivity outcomes.
</div>

The revised statistical question is:

> **Among Australian adults aged 18–65, does physical activity modify the association between two-day mean fibre intake and adiposity, measured using BMI and waist circumference, in the 2011–13 Australian Health Survey?**

This is a deliberate change from the earlier obesity-only analysis. It retains the same exposure → outcome → modifier framing, but avoids discarding information at the BMI 30 kg/m² threshold.

# Why the old analysis changed

The previous draft used binary BMI-defined obesity as the primary outcome and continuous BMI as a sensitivity analysis. The supplied Project 1 tips explicitly advise considering BMI continuously because dichotomisation loses signal. They also identify waist circumference as a potentially more direct marker of central adiposity than BMI. Therefore:

1. **Primary outcome 1:** BMI (kg/m²), continuous.
2. **Primary outcome 2:** waist circumference (cm), continuous and complementary.
3. **Sensitivity outcomes:** BMI-defined obesity (BMI at least 30 kg/m²) and sex-specific elevated waist circumference.
4. The two primary interaction p-values are interpreted together and Holm-adjusted to limit outcome-selection bias.

