# Causal and modelling strategy

The exposure is two-day mean fibre intake. The effect modifier is whether the participant met the 150-minute physical activity guideline. The outcomes are BMI and waist circumference, analysed separately.

The primary adjustment set was selected before examining p-values:

- age, modelled flexibly with a restricted cubic spline basis;
- sex;
- total energy intake;
- household income decile;
- Year 12 completion;
- smoking status;
- alcohol intake.

Physical activity is not treated as an ordinary confounder because it is the prespecified effect modifier and therefore appears as both a main effect and an interaction with fibre. Area-level SEIFA replaces income and education in a sensitivity model. Current dieting, chronic disease and medication use are not automatically adjusted because obesity can influence diagnosis, medication and current dietary behaviour, making their temporal position ambiguous. Broader dietary pattern remains unmeasured.

## Primary continuous interaction models

For outcome \(Y\), where \(Y\) is BMI or waist circumference:

\[
Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1F_c + \beta_2PA + \beta_3(F_c \times PA)
+ \boldsymbol{\gamma}^{T}\boldsymbol{C} + \epsilon,
\]

where

\[
F_c = \frac{\text{two-day mean fibre (g/day)} - 23}{5}.
\]

Therefore:

- \(\beta_1\) is the mean outcome difference per 5 g/day more fibre among participants not meeting the PA guideline;
- \(\beta_2\) compares PA groups at 23 g/day fibre;
- \(\beta_3\) is the formal effect-modification test: the difference between the two fibre slopes.

Day 2 person weights and all 60 Day 2 replicate weights are used. Natural outcome units are primary for interpretation. Standardised outcomes are reported only to compare relative effect sizes across BMI and waist circumference.

```{r fit-primary-models}
primary_covariates <- "ns(age, df = 3) + sex + energy_1000 + income_decile + year12 + smoking + alcohol_10"

bmi_formula <- as.formula(paste(
  "bmi ~ fibre_5g_c * pa +", primary_covariates
))

waist_formula <- as.formula(paste(
  "waist_cm ~ fibre_5g_c * pa +", primary_covariates
))

bmi_model <- fit_replicate_lm(general_primary, bmi_formula, general_repweights)
waist_model <- fit_replicate_lm(general_primary, waist_formula, general_repweights)

bmi_tab <- continuous_table(bmi_model)
waist_tab <- continuous_table(waist_model)

extract_primary_effects <- function(model, outcome_label, unit_label) {
  p <- length(model$beta)
  nms <- names(model$beta)
  c_not <- setNames(rep(0, p), nms)
  c_not["fibre_5g_c"] <- 1
  c_met <- c_not
  c_met["fibre_5g_c:paMet"] <- 1
  c_pa <- setNames(rep(0, p), nms)
  c_pa["paMet"] <- 1
  c_int <- setNames(rep(0, p), nms)
  c_int["fibre_5g_c:paMet"] <- 1

  bind_rows(
    linear_combination(model, c_not, "Fibre slope: PA guideline not met"),
    linear_combination(model, c_met, "Fibre slope: PA guideline met"),
    linear_combination(model, c_pa, "PA difference at 23 g/day fibre"),
    linear_combination(model, c_int, "Fibre × PA interaction")
  ) %>%
    mutate(outcome = outcome_label, unit = unit_label)
}

primary_effects <- bind_rows(
  extract_primary_effects(bmi_model, "BMI", "kg/m²"),
  extract_primary_effects(waist_model, "Waist circumference", "cm")
)

interaction_results <- primary_effects %>%
  filter(effect == "Fibre × PA interaction") %>%
  mutate(holm_p = p.adjust(p_value, method = "holm"))
```

# Primary results

## Interaction estimates in natural units

```{r primary-results-table, echo=FALSE}
primary_effects %>%
  mutate(
    Estimate = sprintf("%.2f", estimate),
    `95% CI` = sprintf("%.2f to %.2f", ci_low, ci_high),
    `p-value` = fmt_p(p_value)
  ) %>%
  dplyr::select(Outcome = outcome, Effect = effect, Estimate, `95% CI`, `p-value`, Unit = unit) %>%
  kable(caption = "Primary survey-weighted continuous interaction models")
```

```{r holm-table, echo=FALSE}
interaction_results %>%
  transmute(
    Outcome = outcome,
    `Interaction estimate` = sprintf("%.2f %s per 5 g/day", estimate, unit),
    `95% CI` = sprintf("%.2f to %.2f", ci_low, ci_high),
    `Raw p-value` = fmt_p(p_value),
    `Holm-adjusted p-value` = fmt_p(holm_p)
  ) %>%
  kable(caption = "Primary interaction tests with multiplicity adjustment across the two outcomes")
```

The interaction estimate is a difference in slopes, not the fibre slope itself. A value near zero means the fibre association is similar in the two PA groups. Statistical evidence for effect modification requires the interaction confidence interval to exclude zero; the Holm-adjusted p-values protect against selectively highlighting whichever of BMI or waist happens to be more favourable.

