# Assumptions and robustness

## Linearity, residuals and influence

Linear regression does not require BMI or waist circumference themselves to be normally distributed. The relevant assumptions concern the conditional mean structure, residual form, influential observations and the adequacy of the variance estimates. Replicate-weight standard errors account for the complex sampling design; the following graphics examine model form rather than replacing survey inference.

```{r residual-diagnostics, echo=FALSE, fig.height=5.3}
diagnostic_data <- bind_rows(
  data.frame(
    fitted = fitted(bmi_model$fit),
    residual = residuals(bmi_model$fit),
    outcome = "BMI"
  ),
  data.frame(
    fitted = fitted(waist_model$fit),
    residual = residuals(waist_model$fit),
    outcome = "Waist circumference"
  )
)

ggplot(diagnostic_data, aes(x = fitted, y = residual)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "#94A3B8") +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.12, size = .8, colour = grey) +
  geom_smooth(method = "loess", se = FALSE, colour = orange, linewidth = 1) +
